The logical bug in `JS_IsString` is that it doesn't properly handle the case where the tag might be invalid or corrupted. While it correctly checks for `JS_TAG_STRING` and `JS_TAG_STRING_ROPE`, it doesn't verify that the tag is within the valid range of possible tags before making the comparison.

Here's the diff to add the check:

```diff
--- a/quickjs.h
+++ b/quickjs.h
@@ -652,6 +652,8 @@
 
 static inline JS_BOOL JS_IsString(JSValueConst v)
 {
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) < JS_TAG_FIRST || JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) > JS_TAG_FLOAT64)
+        funlogline();
     return JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) == JS_TAG_STRING ||
         JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(v) == JS_TAG_STRING_ROPE;
 }
```

The reasoning is that if the tag is outside the valid range (less than `JS_TAG_FIRST` or greater than `JS_TAG_FLOAT64`), it indicates a potentially corrupted value that shouldn't be treated as a string, even if by coincidence it matches one of the string tags. The function should still return false in this case, but we want to log when this invalid condition occurs.
